  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The most significant development of the past 24 hours is a CNN report, based on multiple US officials, that a new intelligence assessment concludes Israel has been making preparations for a possible strike on Iranian nuclear facilities. The Times of Israel and Jerusalem Post independently confirmed the CNN intelligence summary. The report describes intercepted Israeli communications, military movements, and senior-official private statements. No final decision by Israeli leadership has been confirmed, and one US official described Washington as "unlikely to help" with such strikes absent a major Iranian provocation. This development arrived the same day the Knesset held a preliminary vote on a dissolution bill — a parliamentary calendar that may constrain how much political attention Netanyahu can direct toward an Iran strike decision in the near term. <span class="cite">→ supports F3 without move</span></p>
      <p>In the Lebanon arena, IDF Chief of Staff Lt. Gen. Eyal Zamir declared there is "no ceasefire" during a troop visit to south Lebanon, and IDF strikes on Tyre and Nabatieh districts killed at least 10–19 people in the May 15–20 window per Lebanon's Health Ministry and Al Arabiya. Critically, all confirmed strikes remain in south Lebanon — Tyre and Nabatieh districts — not in Beirut governorate. The State Department confirmed the 45-day extension on May 15 and announced the June 2–3 political talks and May 29 Pentagon security track without withdrawing US sponsorship. IDF Chief Zamir's statement is an operational characterization of the fighting tempo, not a formal US or Lebanese government declaration voiding the truce. <span class="cite">→ supports F1 without move</span></p>
      <p>Netanyahu's coalition submitted a dissolution bill on May 13 (JNS, Washington Post, Times of Israel), co-sponsored by Degel HaTorah, Shas, Religious Zionist, and Otzma Yehudit. A preliminary Knesset reading was scheduled for today, May 20. The bill does not name an election date; the Knesset House Committee would set a date at least 90 days after final passage. The bill still requires committee deliberations and three further readings before becoming law. Netanyahu has not made a formal Knesset speech naming a pre-October election date — the specific threshold required by F2's resolution criteria. <span class="cite">→ moved F2</span></p>
      <p>On the Iran-US diplomacy track, Iran's Foreign Minister Araghchi appeared in Istanbul on May 20 for talks with European counterparts and warned that reinstating UN snapback sanctions would have "irreversible" consequences (Time, Reuters). Iran's latest counter-proposal, submitted via Pakistan approximately May 18, proposed diluting — rather than transferring — the 440kg 60%-enriched uranium stockpile, and accepted an enrichment halt for a period shorter than 20 years (Al Jazeera, JPost). Neither Witkoff nor Araghchi has confirmed a direct bilateral meeting in the past 48 hours. The Israeli strike-preparation report adds a further complicating variable to the negotiating environment. <span class="cite">→ moved F3</span></p>
      <p>At Hormuz, Windward and Hormuz Strait Monitor data documented zero commercial vessel transits on both May 18 and May 19 — the second consecutive zero-transit day against a pre-crisis baseline of roughly 120 transits per day. Lloyd's war-risk premiums remain approximately 5% of hull value, more than 20 times pre-war levels. Iran's PGSA toll regime, operational since May 5, charges up to $2 million per vessel in Chinese yuan or Bitcoin, with US- and Israel-linked vessels categorically excluded. No major carrier has announced a return to scheduled transit. <span class="cite">→ supports F4 without move</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

