  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iran's state media framed the past 48 hours as a period of active diplomatic defense rather than concession. IRNA and Tasnim ran prominent coverage of Araghchi's Istanbul appearance on May 20, leading with his warning about "irreversible" consequences if European powers trigger the UN snapback mechanism — language designed to establish a floor on the nuclear file rather than flexibility. Press TV covered the Hormuz PGSA toll collections without naming specific vessel operators, describing the mechanism as the "normalization of Iranian sovereign rights in the strait." State media did not carry the CNN Israel-strike-preparation report directly; Kayhan ran a brief item describing the report as "Zionist psychological warfare," framing it as disinformation rather than a credible threat. Tasnim published Araghchi's direct quote — "Iran will only accept a fair and comprehensive agreement" — as the lead diplomatic framing for today's cycle. The absence of any state-media acknowledgment that Iran's counter-proposal conceded on enrichment duration is notable: the gap between domestic narrative (no concessions) and the actual negotiating position (shorter halt, dilution not transfer) is managed by keeping specific numbers out of Persian-language state coverage entirely. <span class="cite">→ supports F3 without move</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

